    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A wet/dry vac will pull water out of a carpet, a basement floor or a blocked sink faster than anything else you own — and it will ruin a filter in about four seconds if you forget to take it out first. The routine is short, and the safety rule at the start of it is not optional.
        </p>

        <div id="before-you-start" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Before You Switch It On
          </h2>

          <p>
            Picking up water is the capability that separates a wet/dry vac from every other vacuum in the house, and it is genuinely safe — provided the machine is set up for it. Three checks take a minute and prevent both a ruined filter and a dangerous situation.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Confirm the machine is rated wet/dry.</strong> Almost every shop vac is, but a dry-only dust extractor is not, and neither is any household vacuum. If the manual does not say wet pickup, do not.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Remove the pleated cartridge filter.</strong> This is the step people skip and regret. A paper or pleated filter soaks through in seconds, collapses, and is finished — and while it is wet it chokes airflow so completely that the machine barely lifts water at all. If your model uses a foam sleeve for wet work, fit it now.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Take the collection bag out.</strong> Bags are paper or fleece and disintegrate on contact with water, dumping their contents into the tank and blocking the intake.
          </p>
          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">Safety Before Anything Else</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">If water has reached outlets, appliances or a consumer unit, cut the power to that area first.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Standing water near electrical equipment is a genuine electrocution risk, and a shop vac is not the first tool in that situation — the breaker is. Plug the vac into a GFCI-protected outlet outside the wet area, and never stand in the water while operating it.</p>
          </div>

        </div>

        <div id="setup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Setting Up for Wet Pickup
          </h2>

          <p>
            Once the filter is out and the bag is gone, a few choices decide how quickly the job goes.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Use the Wide Nozzle, Not the Brush</h3>
          <p>
            A squeegee or wide utility nozzle moves far more water than a floor brush, whose bristles hold liquid and break the seal against the floor. If your vac came with a rubber-lipped squeegee head, this is the job it exists for.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Keep the Machine Lower Than the Water Where You Can</h3>
          <p>
            Lifting water costs suction. A vac standing at the bottom of basement steps pulling from the floor beside it clears a puddle noticeably faster than the same machine at the top of the stairs pulling water up ten feet of hose. Run a longer hose rather than raising the vac.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Plan Where the Water Goes First</h3>
          <p>
            A full 12-gallon tank holds around a hundred pounds of water. If your machine has a drain port, position it near a floor drain or run a garden hose to one before you start. If it does not, work in shorter cycles and empty into a bucket you can actually carry rather than trying to tip the drum.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Check the Float</h3>
          <p>
            The float shut-off is what stops water reaching the motor when the tank fills. Look into the tank and confirm it moves freely on its cage — floats stick after a job involving anything other than clean water, and a stuck float is the safety device that is not working.
          </p>

        </div>

        <div id="technique" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Technique: Working a Wet Floor Efficiently
          </h2>

          <p>
            Water does not behave like debris, and the instinct to sweep the nozzle around the way you would on dry dust wastes a lot of time.
          </p>

          <p>
            Work from the outside of the puddle inward, keeping the nozzle in contact with the floor so it maintains a seal. Breaking the seal lets the machine pull air instead of water, and you hear it immediately — the note rises and the pickup rate collapses. Slow, deliberate passes with the nozzle held down move far more water per minute than fast sweeping.
          </p>

          <p>
            On carpet, press the nozzle down firmly and pull it toward you slowly, then repeat over the same strip. Carpet holds water in the pile and the backing, and a single pass removes surprisingly little. Expect to make three or four passes over the same area, and expect the last pass to still bring up water.
          </p>

          <p>
            Listen to the machine rather than watching the tank. The sound changes when the nozzle loses contact, when the hose picks up air, and most importantly when the float rises. That change in note is your cue to stop and drain rather than a fault.
          </p>
          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Situation</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Approach</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Expect</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Hard floor puddle</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Squeegee nozzle, outside inward</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Fast — minutes per gallon</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Wet carpet</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Press down, slow repeat passes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Slow; multiple passes needed</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Overflowed sink or bath</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Small nozzle, empty often</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Quick with a small tank</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Flooded basement</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Wide nozzle, drain hose set up first</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Hours; a pump may be faster</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Clogged sink or drain water</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Treat as contaminated, disinfect after</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Clean the tank thoroughly</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

        </div>

        <div id="after" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Cleaning Up Afterwards — The Part That Matters
          </h2>

          <p>
            What you do in the ten minutes after a wet job decides whether the machine smells for the rest of its life. Water left in a shop vac goes sour fast, and the smell comes back every time you switch on.
          </p>

          <p>
            Drain the tank fully, then rinse it with clean water and, for anything from a drain or a toilet overflow, a dilute bleach or disinfectant solution. Wipe it out and leave the lid off so the inside dries completely.
          </p>

          <p>
            Rinse the foam sleeve, squeeze it out, and let it dry separately and fully before it goes back on the machine. Foam is the component that most often grows mildew, and a damp sleeve stored inside a closed vac is the classic recipe for a musty machine.
          </p>

          <p>
            Drain the hose by stretching it out on a slope — water pooled in the corrugations is both a smell source and, next time you use the vac dry, a plug that blocks airflow entirely. Then run the machine for thirty seconds with nothing attached to dry the impeller housing and the air path.
          </p>

          <p>
            Only refit the pleated cartridge once the tank and the machine are dry. Fitting it into a damp vac wicks water straight into the pleats and ruins a perfectly good filter, which is the most common way cartridges die.
          </p>

        </div>

        <div id="limits" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. What a Shop Vac Cannot Do With Water
          </h2>

          <p>
            A wet/dry vac is the right tool for a surprising number of water problems and the wrong one for a few.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Large Volumes</h3>
          <p>
            Anything beyond a few dozen gallons is a pump job, not a vacuum job. A submersible utility pump moves water continuously without stopping to empty, and for a genuinely flooded basement it will finish in a fraction of the time. Use the shop vac for the last half inch the pump cannot reach — that is where it excels.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Contaminated Water</h3>
          <p>
            Sewage backup and floodwater carry biological hazards, and a domestic shop vac is not a remediation tool. The tank, hose and nozzle will all need thorough disinfection afterwards, and category three water is a job for professionals with the right protective equipment.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Hot Liquids and Flammables</h3>
          <p>
            Never vacuum hot water, solvents, fuel or anything flammable. The motor produces sparks at the brushes, and drawing flammable vapour through it is exactly how that becomes a fire. This is a hard limit, not a caution.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Water With Live Electricity Nearby</h3>
          <p>
            Cut the power first. A shop vac does not make standing water around outlets and appliances safe to approach, and no cleanup task is worth that risk.
          </p>

        </div>

        <div id="drying" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Drying the Room Afterwards
          </h2>

          <p>
            Removing the water is half the job. Everything underneath it is still wet, and how quickly it dries decides whether you have a cleanup or a mould problem.
          </p>

          <p>
            Get air moving across the wet surfaces immediately. Fans aimed low and across the floor do far more than a fan pointed at the ceiling, and opening windows helps only if the outside air is drier than the inside — on a humid day it makes things worse.
          </p>

          <p>
            Run a dehumidifier in the space with the doors closed. A wet floor releases moisture into the air for days, and a dehumidifier removes it faster than ventilation in most climates. Our <a href="/best-dehumidifier-for-basement/">basement dehumidifier guide</a> covers sizing for exactly this situation.
          </p>

          <p>
            Lift what you can. Rugs, boxes and anything with a cardboard base should come off the wet floor entirely, because they hold water against the surface and dry far more slowly than the floor itself. Wet carpet underlay is rarely salvageable and holds water long after the carpet above it feels dry.
          </p>

          <p>
            The rule of thumb professionals work to is that materials which stay wet beyond about 48 hours are at real risk of mould growth. That window is why the drying matters as much as the extraction, and why a shop vac plus fans plus a dehumidifier is the complete answer rather than the vacuum alone.
          </p>

        </div>

        <div id="other-wet-jobs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Other Wet Jobs a Shop Vac Handles Well
          </h2>

          <p>
            Once you are comfortable with wet pickup, the machine becomes useful in ways people rarely think of.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Clogged sinks.</strong> A shop vac sealed over a drain opening pulls a blockage upward where a plunger pushes it down, and on a kitchen sink full of standing water it often clears what a plunger will not. Clear the standing water first, then seal the hose against the drain with a rag.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Draining appliances.</strong> A washing machine or dishwasher that will not drain can be emptied enough to move or repair by pulling the water out through the drain hose.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Gutters and downpipes.</strong> Wet leaf sludge in a gutter is exactly what a wet/dry vac is for, and with a curved extension it can be done from a ladder far more safely than by hand.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Aquariums and water features.</strong> For partial water changes, a shop vac reserved for clean use is faster than siphoning.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Snow and slush in a porch or garage.</strong> Unpleasant with anything else, trivial with a wet vac and a squeegee nozzle. Just remember the same rules apply every time: no cartridge filter, no bag, drain and dry afterwards. Our <a href="/best-shop-vac/">shop vac comparison</a> notes which machines have a drain port, which is the single feature that makes all of this pleasant rather than tedious.
          </p>

        </div>


        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving the Pleated Filter In</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The most common and most expensive wet-pickup mistake. The cartridge soaks, collapses and is ruined in one job, and while it is wet the machine hardly picks up at all — leading people to conclude their vac is weak when they have simply blocked it with a wet filter.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Vacuuming Around Live Electricity</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Standing water near outlets, appliances or a panel is an electrocution risk, and the first tool is the breaker, not the vacuum. Cut power to the area, plug the vac into a GFCI outlet outside the wet zone, and never stand in the water while operating it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Filling Past the Maximum Water Line</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The float shut-off needs headroom to seal the intake before water reaches the motor. Filling above the marked line defeats it, and a stuck float defeats it entirely — check the float moves freely on its cage before starting any wet job.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Storing the Machine Wet</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Water left in the tank, the foam sleeve or the hose turns sour within days, and the smell then transfers to everything the vac exhausts. Drain, rinse, dry the sleeve separately, stretch the hose out, and leave the lid off until the inside is dry.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Vacuuming Flammable or Hot Liquids</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The motor sparks at the brushes, so drawing solvent, fuel or flammable vapour through a shop vac is a straightforward fire risk. Hot liquids can also deform the tank. This is a hard limit on the tool, not a technique to be careful with.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a shop vac suck up water?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, if it is a wet/dry model — that is its defining capability. Remove the pleated cartridge filter and the collection bag first, fit the foam sleeve if your model uses one, and do not fill past the maximum water line. Never attempt this with a dry-only vacuum or a household vacuum; liquid reaching the motor is a shock and fire risk.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need to remove the filter to vacuum water?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. A pleated or paper cartridge soaks through in seconds, collapses, and is ruined, and while wet it chokes airflow so completely that the machine barely lifts water. Take it out, fit the foam sleeve if your model has one, and only refit the cartridge once both the tank and the machine are completely dry.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much water can a shop vac hold?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Close to its tank rating, but weight becomes the limit before volume does — a gallon of water weighs over eight pounds, so a 12-gallon tank holds around a hundred pounds. Never fill past the marked maximum water line, because the float shut-off needs headroom to work. A drain port makes large tanks practical.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use a shop vac on wet carpet?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, and it is one of the best uses for one. Press the nozzle down firmly and pull slowly toward you, then repeat over the same strip three or four times — carpet holds water in the pile and the backing, and one pass removes far less than it appears to. Follow with fans and a dehumidifier.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a shop vac unclog a sink?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Often, yes. Sealed over the drain opening with a rag packed around the hose, a wet/dry vac pulls a blockage upward where a plunger pushes it down, and it clears clogs a plunger will not. Remove the standing water first, and disinfect the tank afterwards since drain water is contaminated.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does my shop vac smell after vacuuming water?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Because water was left in it. Drain the tank fully, rinse it, disinfect if the water was dirty, dry the foam sleeve separately and completely, stretch the hose out to drain, and run the machine briefly with nothing attached to dry the air path. Leave the lid off until the inside is dry.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I use a shop vac or a pump for a flooded basement?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A pump for the bulk and a shop vac for the last half inch. A submersible utility pump moves water continuously without stopping to empty, so it finishes a genuine flood in a fraction of the time, but it cannot clear the shallow film a vacuum picks up easily. For a few gallons, the vacuum alone is quicker to set up.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

